    fix(cli): refuse hard-linked targets before writing, and stop rejecting a
    directory merely spelled like the git directory

    Three review findings from trail 117.

    init-agents enforced the hard-link refusal on the WRITE, because an open
    handle is the only thing that can be asked about an inode. But the first
    write that can reach it is the one that creates or overwrites the guide,
    so `AGENTS.md` hard-linked to `.git/config` was refused only after
    `.entire/graph-agent.md` had been written -- and when a user guide was
    already there it was overwritten, which rollback never ran for and could
    not have restored. Ask the same question in the preflight so the refusal
    lands before the first byte. The write-time guard stays: the preflight is
    not the enforcement, and a link swapped in afterwards is still refused.

    The git-directory refusal folded case unconditionally. That fold is
    necessary on macOS and Windows, where a repository-chosen `CLAUDE.md ->
    .GIT/config` really does open `.git/config`. Where the kernel does not
    fold, the reasoning describes nothing: `.GIT` is an ordinary directory no
    git command has used, and a contained `AGENTS.md -> .GIT/rules.md` was
    rejected for its spelling alone. Keep the fold and then check it against
    the filesystem: an exact `.git` component is always refused, a case
    variant only when os.SameFile proves both spellings open the same
    directory, and every unanswerable case keeps the fold's refusal. So this
    can confirm a refusal the name rule raised, never withdraw one it could
    not decide.

    The third finding was a documentation defect, not a code one. The docs
    promised hard links were supported "including one outside the project",
    which trail 117 stopped being true. The guard cannot be narrowed -- an
    inode's other names are not enumerable from a handle, and the danger set
    is every non-instruction file in the repo, not just the git directory --
    so the docs are corrected to match the behaviour instead.
